gin": 2.03, "analyst_adjustment": -0.03, "key_factors": ["Open R+5 seat in a strongly Democratic national environment", "Ager's large fundraising edge over late-entering Balkcom", "Raters nearly unanimous Tossup; markets price Ager a modest favorite", "Helene recovery and FEMA aid dominate the local debate"], "rationale": "The district's five-point Republican lean is offset by a Democratic-tilting national climate and Jamie Ager's clear money advantage over Jennifer Balkcom, who entered the race late. Eight of nine race raters call it a tossup and Kalshi and Polymarket make Ager a modest favorite, consistent with the narrow Democratic edge in the limited polling.", "watch": "Fresh polling, late outside spending, or shifts in the Helene and FEMA aid debate could easily move a race this close.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Western North Carolina's House seat is open after Republican Chuck Edwards retired, and the contest between Democrat Jamie Ager and Republican state Rep. Jennifer Balkcom sits near the center of the battleground map. The district leans Republican by about five points, but the national climate favors Democrats, and nearly every race rater calls the contest a tossup; only Inside Elections tilts it to the right. Kalshi and Polymarket make Ager a modest favorite at roughly 60 to 64 percent.\n\nAger holds a clear money advantage, raising about $3.1 million with $2.2 million on hand, while Balkcom entered the race late and has not reported comparable totals. The one recent poll, a Democratic-sponsored survey, showed Ager up three among registered voters. Hurricane Helene recovery and federal aid remain salient local issues, and both candidates are campaigning heavily on them.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5972, "margin": 1.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6249, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6236, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_